  annotation = {中文辅助译名：使用 LLM 指导的合成数据通过紧凑模型进行个性化睡眠分析的轮廓辅助蒸馏框架},
  abstract = {Introduction: Enabling personalized sleep analysis and interaction directly on edge devices is crucial for providing real-time health insights and tailored guidance. However, this goal remains challenging due to the scarcity of high-quality physiological data and the computational constraints of edge hardware. Methods: We propose a framework for personalized sleep analysis on edge devices that addresses two key obstacles: limited publicly available physiological datasets and the restricted capacity of compact models. To mitigate data scarcity, we introduce a Physiologically-Constrained Adaptive Hierarchical Copula approach, which leverages large language model-guided optimization to synthesize diverse and realistic physiological signals. To enhance personalized inference on resource-limited models, we further develop Profile-Aided Distillation of Expert Inference with MoE LoRA, which integrates user-specific profile information to improve the performance of edge-deployed models. Results: Extensive experiments on both public and in-house datasets show that the distilled models achieve performance comparable to state-of-the-art large language models, while operating efficiently within the computational and memory constraints of edge devices. Discussion: These results demonstrate that the proposed framework offers a practical and effective solution for enabling personalized sleep analysis and user interaction in resource-constrained environments, bridging the gap between high-pe},
}

@misc{panorama_xiaofen_xing_7957ed70d0a3dffe,
  title = {Retrieving Complementary Evidence for Multi-Hop RAG with Semantic Gain Graphs},
  author = {Zeliang Li and Xiaofen Xing and Kailing Guo and Xiangmin Xu},
  year = {2026},
  doi = {10.48550/arxiv.2610.15285},
  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15285},
  howpublished = {arXiv (Cornell University)},
  annotation = {中文辅助译名：使用语义增益图检索多跳 RAG 的补充证据},
  abstract = {Multi-hop retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) requires finding documents whose evidence is useful in combination. Query relevance and graph connectivity help locate related content, but neither directly measures the additional information one document contributes given another. We introduce SG$^2$RAG, a framework that represents this complementarity through semantic gain. Offline, a large language model assesses the relevant and nonredundant information contributed by one chunk given another within neighborhoods proposed by semantic similarity. These ordered assessments are aggregated into symmetric edge weights to construct a reusable Semantic Gain Graph. Online, controlled Personalized PageRank starts from query-relevant seeds and ranks complementary evidence beyond the initial retrieval set. Experiments on MuSiQue, 2Wiki, and HotpotQA with two language model backbones show that SG$^2$RAG achieves the best average EM, F1, and Recall@10 among the evaluated single-retrieval methods. Under identical candidate support and propagation, semantic gain improves average F1 and Recall@10 over similarity weighting by 4.08 and 4.31 points. Analyses of supporting documents and improvements in three iterative retrieval frameworks further support its effectiveness as a reusable retrieval component.},
}

@misc{panorama_xiaofen_xing_faa140658f9ed4ba,
  title = {Gradient-Modulated Cross-Entropy Loss for Robust Learning With Label Noise},
  author = {Lin Wang and Fang Liu and Xiangmin Xu and Kailing Guo and Xiaofen Xing and Zhanpeng Jin},
  year = {2026},
  doi = {10.1109/tnnls.2026.3739825},
  url = {https://doi.org/10.1109/tnnls.2026.3739825},
  howpublished = {IEEE Transactions on Neural Networks and Learning Systems},
  annotation = {中文辅助译名：梯度调制交叉熵损失用于标签噪声的鲁棒学习},
  abstract = {Deep neural networks (DNNs) trained with the standard cross-entropy (CE) loss typically achieve strong performance on clean datasets but suffer from severe overfitting when confronted with label noise. To alleviate this issue, numerous robust loss functions have been proposed and shown to improve noise tolerance. However, most existing methods focus predominantly on the target class, overlooking informative class-structural cues embedded in nontarget predictions. In this article, we propose a simple yet effective loss function, named gradient-modulated CE (GMCE), which enhances robustness by incorporating uncertainty-aware gradient modulation across the entire predicted distribution. GMCE comprises two synergistic components: negative-shifted CE (NSCE) loss and uncertainty-aware calibration mechanism (UACM). Specifically, NSCE attenuates the gradient magnitude for highly uncertain predictions by applying a convexity-preserving negative shift to the CE loss, thereby flattening its curvature in low-confidence regions. In parallel, UACM adaptively reweights the loss magnitude based on the entropy and cumulative probability over nontarget classes, assigning greater emphasis to samples with peaked and confident output distributions. Furthermore, we provide a theoretical analysis demonstrating the robustness of GMCE under label noise. Extensive experiments on benchmark datasets with both synthetic and real-world label noise, including MNIST, CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, WebVision, and Clot},
}

@misc{panorama_xiaofen_xing_73f13f071e8b8653,
  title = {Beyond Similarity through Zero-Token Geometric Graphs for Multi-Hop RAG},
  author = {Zeliang Li and Xiaofen Xing and Kailing Guo and Wenyu Tao and Xiangmin Xu},
  year = {2026},
  doi = {10.48550/ARXIV.2609.19622},
  url = {https://doi.org/10.48550/ARXIV.2609.19622},
  howpublished = {CoRR},
  annotation = {中文辅助译名：通过多跳 RAG 的零令牌几何图超越相似性},
  abstract = {Existing Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems struggle with multi-hop reasoning due to a fundamental inability to balance relevance and novelty. Traditional dense retrievers are constrained by surface-level matching, falling into a homogenized "Similarity Trap". Conversely, emerging Graph RAGs attempt to explore novel documents via heuristic entity extraction, but they introduce prohibitive Large Language Model (LLM) overhead and easily drift into off-topic "Novelty Traps". To fundamentally resolve this contradiction, we propose Geometric Gain Graph (G$^3$RAG), the novel zero-token graph construction paradigm for RAG. G$^3$RAG completely discards the expensive heuristic extraction by LLMs, aiming instead to directly quantify the information gain between nodes through the native document feature space. To this end, we designed a concise edge weight criterion, $\cosθ\cdot \sinθ$, which naturally maps the trade-off between relevance and novelty into a geometric measurement of directional consistency and orthogonality in the vector space, thereby driving the token-free topological construction of the graph. Furthermore, we introduce a topological penalty to suppress the excessive connectivity of high-frequency hubs, forcing the transient diffusion process toward long-tail peripheral nodes that carry critical indirect evidence. Extensive experiments demonstrate that, while entirely eliminating LLM token costs during graph construction, G$^3$RAG significantly outperforms st},
}

